{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788386006,"nonce":"GKTfFcB+InTI+a7t7GyQogbE2/I=","message":"Concrete case for list B (\"should the MOD just do this?\"), observed in play\nminutes after this issue was opened. Dorian flagged it: **tending a downed\ncolonist should be mechanical and is not.**\n\n\nAaron was downed by a manhunting ostrich (day 8 Summer, tick 700,058). The\nmod's casualty machinery worked perfectly up to the last step:\n\n- `advance` halted on `reason: \"casualty\"` with `halted_on` naming the pawn,\n  the damage and the tick. Correct.\n- `triage` returned the whole answer in one call — bleed clock 18,180 ticks,\n  `margin_ticks` 17,395, every candidate rescuer with travel time\n  (Ellis 161, Fitz 273, Walton 1,095), a verdict of `in-time`, and `act` as a\n  ready-to-send `rescue {pawn:1022, target:1018}` envelope. This is the best\n  thing on the verb surface.\n- `rescue` forced the job and worked first time.\n\nThen it stopped being mechanical. To actually get him TENDED:\n\n    work-priorities  (Doctor 3 -\u003e 1 on Ellis, 4 -\u003e 2 on Walton)\n    orders {pawn:1014, thing:1018}      -\u003e DoctorTendEmergency available\n    prioritize {pawn:1014, work:\"DoctorTendEmergency\", thing:1018}\n    advance 2000\n    prioritize ... again          \u003c- bleed 3.28 -\u003e 2.08, still needs_tend\n    advance 2000\n    prioritize ... again\n    ...\n\n**One forced tend treats one wound.** A pawn with several injuries needs the\ncall repeated, with an advance between each, and the agent polling\n`pawn.health.bleed_rate` to know when to stop. That is a loop of LLM calls\nwhose every step is decided by arithmetic the mod already has.\n\n\n1. **`one-doctor-is-zero-doctors`, in its purest form.** Aaron was Doctor\n   priority 1 — and Aaron was the patient. `WorkGiver_DoBill.ShouldSkip`\n   requires `billGiver != pawn`, so the colony's only Medicine-4 pawn could\n   not treat himself and everyone else sat at 3 or 4. `digest.work_coverage`\n   reported `Doctor: floor 2, have 3, ok: true` throughout — because it counts\n   AVAILABILITY, and a downed doctor is still \"available\" by that test. The\n   check that exists specifically for this lesson did not fire on the exact\n   case the lesson was written about.\n2. **Nothing woke the doctor.** Walton was asleep. The fix was a forced job,\n   which interrupts sleep — but the agent had to know that, and had to find\n   the work-giver name through `orders` first because `tend` itself refuses\n   (`\"this order is only offered to a drafted pawn\"`).\n\n\n- **`triage`'s `act` should cover the whole casualty, not the first step.**\n  It already computes rescuer, path and clock. Let it return the ordered chain\n  — rescue, then tend, tend, tend until `needs_tend` is false — or expose a\n  `tend-until-stable {patient}` that runs to completion and reports what it\n  did.\n- **`advance --until` needs a casualty-resolved matcher** (list A above):\n  `--until.condition {path:\"colonists.needs_tend\", op:\"==\", value:0}` or\n  `--until.stable \u003cpawn\u003e`. Right now the caller polls.\n- **`work_coverage`'s Doctor row must exclude the patient.** \"Have 3\" is wrong\n  when one of the three is the one bleeding. The floor exists because of this\n  lesson; the denominator should honour it.\n- When a forced job is the only route because the worker is asleep, say so —\n  `prioritize` could report `woke: true` rather than leaving it implicit.\n\nThis is the same shape as `bills[].health`/`remedy`: the mod knows the whole\nanswer, so the mod should hand over the answer, not the ingredients.","files":null}]}